%I A401060 #25 Oct 16 2026 19:05:37
%S A401060 13,19,41,61,67,131,157,167,179,181,211,233,307,337,367,373,409,467,
%T A401060 479,557,673,751,757,797,809,811,853,929,971,1033,1049,1051,1061,1063,
%U A401060 1151,1259,1307,1361,1409,1427,1433,1451,1553,1619,1627,1669,1697,1759,1801,1811,1871,1951,2063,2273,2287,2341,2411,2467
%N A401060 Primes p for which there exists an odd prime q < p such that ((p#/p) / (q#/q)) == -1 mod p, where p# = A034386(p).
%C A401060 Primes p = prime(1+k) such that 1+(A002110(k)/A002110(i)) is a multiple of prime(1+k) for some i, 1 <= i < k.
%C A401060 Primes p = prime(1+k) such that there exists a "cut primorial" x = prime(k) * prime(k-1) * prime(k-2) * ... * prime(i), with k > i >= 2, such that 1+x is a multiple of prime(1+k).
%C A401060 Terms present in A003961 o A058233 (3, 19, 1471, 3001, ...?) but missing from here are at least 3, 1471 and 3001.
%H A401060 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401060/b401060.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..3796</a>
%H A401060 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%e A401060 3 is NOT a term, because there is no odd prime q less than 3 such that 1 + ((3#/3) / (q#/q)) would be a multiple of 3.
%e A401060 19 is a term because 1 + ((19#/19) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 19 when we set q to 11, as then A002110(A000720(19)-1)/A002110(A000720(11)-1) = 510510/210 = 2431 and 2432 = 2^7 * 19.
%e A401060 See also examples in A401061.
%o A401060 (PARI)
%o A401060 A002110(n) = prod(i=1, n, prime(i));
%o A401060 is_A401060_aux(k) = { my(p=prime(1+k)); for(i=1,k-1,if(!((1+(A002110(k)/A002110(i)))%p),return(1))); (0); };
%o A401060 is_A401060_on_p(p) = is_A401060_aux(primepi(p)-1);
%o A401060 (PARI) is_A401060_on_p(p) = { my(n=primepi(p)-1, pr=Mod(1, p)); forstep(k=n, 2, -1, pr *= Mod(prime(k), p); if((p-1)==lift(pr), return(1))); (0); };
%Y A401060 Cf. A002110, A034386.
%Y A401060 Subsequence of A401061.
%Y A401060 Cf. also  A400842, A401063.
%K A401060 nonn,new
%O A401060 1,1
%A A401060 _Antti Karttunen_, Oct 16 2026
